Let $x,y,$ and $z$ be real numbers satisfying the system of equations
\begin{align*} xy + 4z &= 60 \\ yz + 4x &= 60 \\ zx + 4y &= 60. \end{align*}
Systems like this one, which are unchanged under cyclically permuting the variables, are common in olympiad training. Let $S$ be the set of possible values of $x.$ Find the sum of the squares of the elements of $S.$

Let $a,b,$ and $c$ be real numbers satisfying the system of equations
\begin{align*} ab + 2c &= 15 \\ bc + 2a &= 15 \\ ca + 2b &= 15. \end{align*}
Let $T$ be the set of possible values of $2a.$ Find the sum of the squares of the elements of $T.$

Let $a,b,$ and $c$ be real numbers satisfying the system of equations
\begin{align*} ab + 4c &= 60 \\ bc + 4a &= 60 \\ ca + 4b &= 60. \end{align*}
Let $T$ be the set of possible values of $a.$ Find the sum of the squares of the elements of $T.$

Real numbers $x,y,$ and $z$ satisfy the three equations
\begin{align*} xy + 4z &= 60 \\ yz + 4x &= 60 \\ zx + 4y &= 60. \end{align*}
Consider every value that $x$ can take; let $S$ denote the set of these values. Compute the sum of the squares of the members of $S.$

Consider the following $100$ sets of $10$ elements each:
\begin{align*} &\{1,2,3,\ldots,10\}, \\ &\{11,12,13,\ldots,20\},\\ &\{21,22,23,\ldots,30\},\\ &\vdots\\ &\{991,992,993,\ldots,1000\}. \end{align*}
The sum of the elements of the first set is $55$, and each later set has an element sum $100$ larger than the set before it. How many of these sets contain exactly two multiples of $7$?

Consider the following $100$ sets $S_0, S_1, \ldots, S_{99}$ of $10$ elements each:
\begin{align*} S_0&=\{1,2,3,\ldots,10\}, \\ S_1&=\{11,12,13,\ldots,20\},\\ S_2&=\{21,22,23,\ldots,30\},\\ &\vdots\\ S_{99}&=\{991,992,993,\ldots,1000\}. \end{align*}
How many of these sets contain exactly two multiples of $7$?

The integers from $1$ to $1000$ are split into $100$ blocks of $10$ consecutive numbers each:
\begin{align*} &\{1,2,3,\ldots,10\}, \\ &\{11,12,13,\ldots,20\},\\ &\{21,22,23,\ldots,30\},\\ &\vdots\\ &\{991,992,993,\ldots,1000\}. \end{align*}
In how many of these blocks are there exactly two multiples of $7$?

Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be positive real numbers satisfying the system of equations:
\begin{align*} \sqrt{2x-xy} + \sqrt{2y-xy} &= 1 \\ \sqrt{2y-yz} + \sqrt{2z-yz} &= \sqrt2 \\ \sqrt{2z-zx} + \sqrt{2x-zx} &= \sqrt3. \end{align*} 
Systems of this shape were popular in olympiad training sets of the $1990$s. Then $\left[ (1-x)(1-y)(1-z) \right]^2$ can be written as $\frac{m}{n},$ where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $m+n.$

Three sensors in a laboratory return positive real readings $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ A calibration check shows that the readings satisfy
\begin{align*} \sqrt{2x-xy} + \sqrt{2y-xy} &= 1 \\ \sqrt{2y-yz} + \sqrt{2z-yz} &= \sqrt2 \\ \sqrt{2z-zx} + \sqrt{2x-zx} &= \sqrt3. \end{align*} 
The engineers compute the quantity $\left[ (1-x)(1-y)(1-z) \right]^2,$ which can be written as $\frac{m}{n},$ where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $m+n.$

Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be positive real numbers satisfying the system of equations:
\begin{align*} \sqrt{x(2-y)} + \sqrt{y(2-x)} &= 1 \\ \sqrt{y(2-z)} + \sqrt{z(2-y)} &= 2^{1/2} \\ \sqrt{z(2-x)} + \sqrt{x(2-z)} &= 3^{1/2}. \end{align*} 
Then $\left[ (x-1)(y-1)(z-1) \right]^2$ can be written as $\frac{m}{n},$ where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $m+n.$

Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers satisfying the system of equations:
\begin{align*} \sqrt{2a-ab} + \sqrt{2b-ab} &= 1 \\ \sqrt{2b-bc} + \sqrt{2c-bc} &= \sqrt2 \\ \sqrt{2c-ca} + \sqrt{2a-ca} &= \sqrt3. \end{align*} 
Then $\left[ (1-a)(1-b)(1-c) \right]^2$ can be written as $\frac{p}{q},$ where $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $p+q.$

The positive real numbers $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ satisfy the following system:
\begin{align*} \sqrt{2x-xy} + \sqrt{2y-xy} &= 1 \\ \sqrt{2y-yz} + \sqrt{2z-yz} &= \sqrt2 \\ \sqrt{2z-zx} + \sqrt{2x-zx} &= \sqrt3. \end{align*} 
If $\left[ (1-x)(1-y)(1-z) \right]^2 = \frac{m}{n}$ for relatively prime positive integers $m$ and $n,$ find $m+n.$

Find the number of triples of nonnegative integers \((a,b,c)\) satisfying \(a + b + c = 300\) and
\begin{equation*}
a^2b + a^2c + b^2a + b^2c + c^2a + c^2b = 6,000,000.
\end{equation*}
A student first tried to check candidate triples by hand, managing about 50 triples per hour before giving up.

Find the number of triples of nonnegative integers \((a,b,c)\) satisfying \(a + b + c = 300\) and
\begin{equation*}
100(ab+bc+ca) - abc = 2,000,000.
\end{equation*}

Find the number of triples of nonnegative integers \((x,y,z)\) satisfying \(x + y + z = 300\) and
\begin{equation*}
x^2y + x^2z + y^2x + y^2z + z^2x + z^2y = 6,000,000.
\end{equation*}

How many triples \((a,b,c)\) of nonnegative integers have \(a+b+c=300\) and also satisfy
\begin{equation*}
a^2b + a^2c + b^2a + b^2c + c^2a + c^2b = 6,000,000?
\end{equation*}
